Consider expressions formed from a base differential field (e.g., the field of rational functions in one variable $x$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, equipped with the derivation $d/dx$) by iterating the formation of indefinite integrals. Concretely, define the class $\mathcal{E}$ of nested-integral expressions recursively:
(i) every element of the base field belongs to $\mathcal{E}$;
(ii) if $f_1,\ldots,f_k\in\mathcal{E}$ and $g$ is a rational function in $k$ variables over the base field, then $g(f_1,\ldots,f_k)\in\mathcal{E}$;
(iii) if $f\in\mathcal{E}$, then the formal indefinite integral $\int f\,dx$ belongs to $\mathcal{E}$.
Define the nesting depth $\mathrm{depth}(E)$ of an expression $E\in\mathcal{E}$ to be $0$ if $E$ is in the base field, $\max_i \mathrm{depth}(f_i)$ if $E=g(f_1,\ldots,f_k)$, and $1+\mathrm{depth}(f)$ if $E=\int f\,dx$. Two expressions are called equivalent if they represent the same element under the natural interpretation as antiderivatives in a suitable differential extension.

Design an algorithm that, given any $E\in\mathcal{E}$, produces an equivalent expression $E'\in\mathcal{E}$ whose nesting depth $\mathrm{depth}(E')$ is minimal among all expressions equivalent to $E$.

